There are continuous functors $f:A\to(B')^\vee$ and $f':A'\to B^\vee$, with natural isomorphisms $$\label{eq:G2}
 \begin{aligned}
 R&\simeq(M')^\vee f,&\qquad (S')^\vee&\simeq fL,\\
 R'&\simeq M^\vee f',& S^\vee&\simeq f'L'.
 \end{aligned}$$ They are uniquely determined, together with these isomorphisms, by <a href="#eq:G1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:G1">[eq:G1]</a> and the fully faithful transposes of localization.

